atching records — all purely **NaN sign-bit** differences (`7fc00000` vs `ffc00000`). Cause: when *both* operands of an add/sub/mul are NaN, x86 returns the *destination* operand, and LLVM commutes operands differently than GCC (it rewrote `l - 0.5*c` into `(-0.5*c) + l`). Note the C library is itself inconsistent here: `-O0` and `-O2` builds of `c_src` disagree with each other on those same NaN cases. I matched the build the provided CMakeLists produces (no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` ⇒ `-O0`), which I confirmed is bit-identical to `gcc -O0`, by routing arithmetic through small `ss_add/ss_sub/ss_mul/ss_div` helpers that spell out the hardware NaN-propagation order taken from the reference disassembly. Non-NaN results are plain IEEE ops and were never affected.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (hashes recorded, `git status` clean for that path)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1127,"cache_read_input_tokens":55940,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1127,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52491607-ddef-4a09-ae1e-ad0d200f2ab9","uuid":"7839e277-e0a8-428b-8a6b-a2aea3df9c17","timestamp":"2026-08-19T10:22:00.538Z"}
